        <body>
            <div xml:id="d259">

                <pb facs="0440" n="344" sameAs="frus:frus1898/pg344"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. White to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-01-02"><hi rendition="#italic">January 2,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 690.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to Mr. Jackson’s dispatch, No.
                    667, of the 19th ultimo, I have the honor to inform you that in No. 162 of the
                    Berliner Correspondenz <pb facs="0441" n="345" xml:id="pg345"/> (officially
                    published by the Prussian ministry of the interior) of the 31st ultimo the
                    following notice appears: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Also in other ports besides Hamburg has the presence of San Jose scale
                            been determined in shipments of fruit from America. The scale has been
                            found in Stettin in dried California pears, moreover at Hamburg in a
                            number of instances in dried pears and nectarines, as well as in fresh
                            Newton Pippins and Ben Davis apples, the shipments being partially
                            strongly infected. They were held up, and their reshipment abroad, under
                            official control, has been ordered.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">And. D. White.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
